The story of Thomas Royan’s 2014 proof of the Gaussian correlation inequality—a 50-year-old math problem cracked while brushing his teeth—provides a masterclass in how human cognitive filters and sociological gatekeeping can cause a correct, monumental proof to be completely ignored.

Here are the five warning signs from the case study that made a correct, groundbreaking proof look like just another crank submission to busy experts:

The Five Warning Signs That Made a Correct Proof Look Wrong
It tackled a famous, long-standing problem:
The Gaussian correlation inequality had resisted elite specialists for over half a century (since 1959/1972). In the math community, papers claiming to solve famous, unbeaten conjectures by outsiders are overwhelmingly incorrect.

The author was an unknown outsider:
Thomas Royan was a 67-year-old retired statistician from a small technical university in Bingen, Germany (Fachhochschule Bingen), rather than a tenured professor at an elite institution known for theoretical probability.

The title and length were disarmingly simple:
The paper was short and titled literally "A simple proof of the Gaussian correlation inequality." Breakthroughs in famous problems are typically expected to be dense, highly complex, and long.

It was typed in Microsoft Word:
Because Royan never learned LaTeX (the typesetting standard used by mathematicians for complex formulas), he drafted and posted the paper in Microsoft Word, making its formatting immediately stand out as unconventional.

It was published in an obscure journal:
Rather than submitting to a top-tier journal (where peer review takes years), Royan posted it to arXiv and published it in the Far East Journal of Theoretical Statistics—a journal most Western experts had never heard of, where Royan coincidentally appeared on the editorial board.

Why This Matters in the Age of AI Proof Generation
As AI-generated proofs and automated theorem provers (like Lean, OpenAI models, and DeepSeek-Prover) produce mathematical manuscripts at an accelerating scale, the bottleneck has shifted entirely from generation to attention and verification.

When mathematician Boaz Barak was emailed Royan’s paper alongside two bogus proofs, he opened one fake, found an error, and set the rest aside due to a simple lack of time. In an era where AI can flood preprint servers with hundreds of proofs, human gatekeeping will increasingly fail not because math is wrong, but because attention is scarce, proving that rigorous formal verification (such as machine-checked codebases) must complement human sociological filters.

